<p>For forty years, from 1984 to 2024, the highest-denomination banknote in Japan — the 10,000-yen bill — carried the face of a man who was never an emperor, a general, or a politician. He was a writer, an educator, and above all a <strong>self-taught language learner</strong>. His name was Fukuzawa Yukichi, and the story of how a low-ranking samurai's son with no formal education until age fourteen used Dutch, English, and an ocean of books to drag an entire nation into the modern world is one of the most extraordinary language-learning stories ever told.</p>

<p>He began his education at fourteen, in a feudal system that legally sealed his social rank at birth. He taught himself two European languages with no teachers and no internet, crossed the Pacific and the Atlantic to study foreign societies firsthand, and then wrote the books that millions of Japanese read — books that helped end feudalism, found one of Japan's greatest universities, and shape the modern nation itself. If you are learning Japanese, the man on the old 10,000-yen note is your ancestor in that endeavor.</p>

<h2>The Lowest Rank of the Samurai Class</h2>

<p>Fukuzawa Yukichi was born on January 10, 1835, in Osaka, into a family of the Okudaira clan of Nakatsu, a castle town in Kyushu. His father, Hyakusuke, was a Confucian scholar who served as the domain's treasury overseer — an intellectual in a bureaucracy that barely paid him. His family's stipend was just over thirteen <em>koku</em> of rice, the smallest possible allowance for a samurai household, and worse, they belonged to the <em>lowest</em> rank of the samurai class.</p>

<p>In Tokugawa Japan, rank was not earned; it was born. Society was divided by law into samurai, peasants, artisans, and merchants, and within the samurai class itself, lower samurai had to speak in honorific language, use a different accent, and sometimes prostrate themselves on the ground when an upper samurai passed. The Fukuzawa family lived in a district far from the castle — a physical marker of their low standing. Promotion beyond one's inherited rank was virtually impossible; Fukuzawa later wrote that a man of his rank had as much chance of advancement as <em>a "four-legged beast [might] hope to fly like a bird."</em></p>

<p>When Fukuzawa's father died in 1836, the family returned to Nakatsu with their income cut further. As the younger son, Fukuzawa would not inherit the family headship or stipend — that went to his older brother. His future options, in the eyes of the system, were adoption into another family, priesthood, or odd jobs. The rigid caste that trapped him planted a resentment he carried for life: he later said he hated the feudal system <em>"as though it were my father's murderer."</em></p>

<blockquote>"Heaven never created a man above another nor a man below another." — opening line of <em>Gakumon no Susume</em> (An Encouragement of Learning), 1872</blockquote>

<h2>Education Begins at Fourteen</h2>

<p>Fukuzawa did not begin formal schooling until he was fourteen — and even then, the education on offer was stratified. Upper samurai studied Confucian classics and military strategy; lower samurai like him were taught practical writing and arithmetic. But Fukuzawa, encouraged by his liberal-minded mother, threw himself into the classics and quickly distinguished himself, leading discussions and debates on Chinese philosophy with an intensity that marked everything he did.</p>

<p>He also absorbed a habit from his father's world: reading as a way of life. Yet he increasingly saw the Confucian curriculum as a dead end — endless commentary on ancient texts, disconnected from the world he could sense changing around him. He wanted out of the "narrow stiffness" of Nakatsu. The escape route appeared in 1853, when Commodore Matthew Perry's American "Black Ships" steamed into Edo Bay and shattered two centuries of Japan's self-imposed isolation. Overnight, the closed country had a Western problem — and Western languages.</p>

<h2>Nagasaki and the Dutch Language Gamble</h2>

<p>In February 1854, at nineteen, Fukuzawa left Nakatsu for Nagasaki — one of the only places in secluded Japan with contact to the West. There he studied <em>Rangaku</em> (Dutch studies), the language through which Japan had been receiving Western knowledge for a century, along with the operation of modern firearms. He progressed quickly, but his success provoked the jealousy of his patron, who fabricated a story that Fukuzawa's mother was ill to force him home.</p>

<p>Seeing through the ruse, Fukuzawa refused to return to Nakatsu. Instead he traveled to Osaka, where his brother persuaded him to enroll at the <strong>Tekijuku</strong>, the school of the physician and scholar Ogata Kōan. The Tekijuku was a pressure cooker of self-education: a handful of students, working with what one observer called "fanatical zeal," teaching themselves physics, chemistry, and medicine from the few Dutch books available in all of Japan. There were no native speakers to practice with. They read, translated, and debated in a language none of them had ever heard spoken.</p>

<p>Fukuzawa became the school's head student. When his brother died in 1856 and he was forced home to head the family, he found the life of a provincial samurai unbearable — and after three months, he obtained the domain lord's permission to return to the Tekijuku, books in hand, for good.</p>

<h2>The Day His First Language Became Obsolete</h2>

<p>In 1858 the Nakatsu domain ordered Fukuzawa to move to Edo (modern Tokyo) and open a Dutch-studies school for the domain's samurai. Shortly after arriving, he visited the new treaty port of Yokohama — and was shocked. The Westerners there, he discovered, spoke <strong>English</strong>, not Dutch. The language he had poured years into was, overnight, the wrong one.</p>

<p>Most people would have despaired. Fukuzawa did what he would do for the rest of his life: he pivoted, immediately and completely. <strong>He began teaching himself English with no teacher, no audio, and no exchange partners</strong> — using his Dutch as a bridge, working from dictionaries and whatever printed material he could obtain. He later compiled one of Japan's first English reference works, an expanded edition of a Chinese-English vocabulary book, published in 1860.</p>

<p>This is the moment that defines his genius as a learner: he never treated languages as an end in themselves. They were tools, and when the tool became obsolete, he learned the new one. Within two years, his self-taught English was good enough to earn him a place on one of the most consequential voyages in Japanese history.</p>

<h2>San Francisco, 1860: The First Japanese in America</h2>

<p>In 1860, the shogunate sent its first diplomatic mission to the United States. Determined to be part of it, Fukuzawa volunteered for the <em>Kanrin Maru</em>, a Dutch-built ship crewed entirely by Japanese sailors serving as the mission's escort. He was accepted as the personal steward of the ship's captain, Kimura Yoshitake.</p>

<p>The voyage was harrowing, and San Francisco overwhelmed most of the delegation — but Fukuzawa treated America as a laboratory. He was not surprised by Western technology, which he already understood from his Dutch books; what struck him were the social details: how Americans behaved, how money moved, how men and women danced together at parties. He asked questions obsessively, noted everything, and returned to Japan in June 1860 to be hired by the shogunate as a translator of diplomatic documents.</p>

<p>In 1862 he went further, joining the shogunate's first mission to Europe as an official translator. Over the course of a year, the mission traveled through France, Britain, the Netherlands, Prussia, Russia, and Portugal. In London, Fukuzawa visited the 1862 International Exhibition, the Woolwich Arsenal, and the Houses of Parliament — and he inquired, tirelessly, about hospitals, banks, the postal system, and how parliaments worked. He was not collecting tourist impressions. He was collecting institutions.</p>

<blockquote>"I spent half of every day studying the institutions of Europe — the schools, the banks, the parliaments — because I knew Japan would need them all." — Fukuzawa Yukichi, on the 1862 mission (paraphrased from his autobiography)</blockquote>

<h2>The School That Became Keio University</h2>

<p>Fukuzawa returned to a Japan sliding into civil war between the shogunate and the imperial forces. He had founded a small school of Dutch studies in Edo in 1858; as the fighting intensified, he refused to stop teaching. In 1868 — while the Battle of Ueno raged through the city — Fukuzawa bought a large estate in the Mita district, moved his school there, renamed it <strong>Keio Gijuku</strong>, and calmly continued lecturing on Francis Wayland's <em>Elements of Political Economy</em> while cannons fired nearby. That school is now Keio University, one of Japan's most prestigious institutions of higher learning.</p>

<p>When the Meiji Restoration triumphed in 1868, the new government repeatedly offered Fukuzawa positions — ministries, advisory roles, honors. He declined them all. He believed that an independent, privately educated citizenry mattered more than any official title, and he chose to remain a writer and educator outside the state. It was an extraordinary decision for a man of his background, and it made him the most trusted voice in Japan.</p>

<h2>An Encouragement of Learning: The Book That Changed a Nation</h2>

<p>Fukuzawa's first great work, <em>Seiyō Jijō</em> (Conditions in the West, 1866–1879), described Western politics, economics, and society in plain Japanese and sold around 250,000 copies — an astronomical figure for the time. But his masterpiece was <em>Gakumon no Susume</em> (<strong>An Encouragement of Learning</strong>, 1872–1876), a series of pamphlets whose opening line — "Heaven never created a man above another nor a man below another" — became the rallying cry of an era.</p>

<p>His argument was radical for feudal Japan: <strong>social status is not written in heaven. It is created by education.</strong> He urged Japanese people to abandon the Confucian habit of deference and instead study practical, scientific knowledge (<em>jitsugaku</em>) — accounting, medicine, engineering, foreign languages — because knowledge, not birth, was the new source of worth. The pamphlets sold in the millions and directly influenced Japan's 1872 Fundamental Code of Education, which established universal schooling. In 1875 he followed with <em>An Outline of a Theory of Civilization</em>, a scholarly analysis of why societies progress, and co-founded the Meirokusha, the intellectual society at the heart of the "Civilization and Enlightenment" movement.</p>

<p>He also founded Japan's first major modern newspaper, <em>Jiji Shinpō</em>, in 1882, and wrote constantly — more than sixty books over his lifetime. His advocacy extended to causes ahead of his time: he condemned concubinage, argued for monogamous marriage, and insisted women deserved real education. (His legacy here is honestly mixed: he limited his advocacy to the home, and his own daughters received a traditional upbringing — a contradiction biographers still wrestle with.)</p>

<h2>What Language Learners Can Learn from Fukuzawa</h2>

<p>Fukuzawa's methods hold up remarkably well against modern learning science:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Learn the tool before you need it.</strong> He mastered Dutch, then English, before his country's fate demanded either. Strategic learners acquire skills ahead of the moment that requires them.</li>
  <li><strong>Pivot without sentimentality.</strong> When Dutch became obsolete, he switched to English within days of the realization. His loyalty was to the goal — understanding the West — not to the language he had already invested in.</li>
  <li><strong>Use the language to get something real.</strong> Fukuzawa never studied Dutch for the sake of Dutch. He studied physics, medicine, and gunnery <em>through</em> it. Languages learned as keys to a subject stick better than languages learned as subjects.</li>
  <li><strong>Read voraciously and deliberately.</strong> His fluency came from thousands of pages of real content — books on science, law, and politics — not from drills. He treated every document as both knowledge and language practice.</li>
  <li><strong>Output constantly.</strong> He translated official documents, compiled dictionaries, and wrote books from the very beginning of his learning. He did not wait until he was "ready" to produce.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Complex Legacy</h2>

<p>Fukuzawa's legacy is not without shadows. In his later years, influenced by the Social Darwinism of the age, he argued that military strength, not international law, secured a nation's survival ("a few cannons are worth more than a hundred volumes of international law"), and his 1885 editorial "Datsu-A Ron" (Leaving Asia) urged Japan to align with the West and distance itself from its Asian neighbors. He supported the First Sino-Japanese War. The imperial expansion of twentieth-century Japan claimed his ideas as intellectual cover, and modern historians rightly debate this part of his record.</p>

<p>But the core of his achievement stands. He suffered his first stroke in 1898, and it was during his recovery that he dictated his <em>Autobiography of Fukuzawa Yukichi</em> — still a classic of Japanese literature and one of the great documents of self-education in any language. He died on February 3, 1901, and his funeral drew thousands; the Lower House of the Diet passed an unprecedented resolution honoring his contributions to the nation.</p>

<p>In 1984, Japan placed his portrait on the 10,000-yen note, the country's highest denomination — the face of a man who began school at fourteen, taught himself two foreign languages with no teachers, and used them to help build the modern Japan. The note carried his image for four decades, until 2024. No banknote has ever carried a better advertisement for the power of learning a language.</p>

<p>When you open your flashcard deck today — especially if it is Japanese — you are standing in a tradition Fukuzawa Yukichi helped create. He would approve of the flashcards. He would approve even more of the fact that you are learning the language to reach something real beyond it: the books, the films, the people, the world.</p>
